(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-resins-ezetimibe-pcsk9-quiz"]={"title":"Resins, Ezetimibe & PCSK9 Inhibitors Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When resins deplete the bile acid pool, which hepatic enzyme increases to convert cholesterol into bile acids?","choices":["Acyl-CoA:cholesterol acyltransferase","Cholesterol 7-alpha hydroxylase","Lipoprotein lipase in plasma","Hepatic HMG-CoA reductase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increased 7-alpha hydroxylase activity converts more cholesterol to bile acids, and the liver adds LDL receptors, increasing VLDL and LDL removal. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the most serious adverse reaction to evolocumab?","choices":["Hypersensitivity reactions","Rhabdomyolysis from muscle breakdown","Prostaglandin-mediated flushing","Gallstones (cholelithiasis)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity reactions are the most serious adverse reaction listed for the PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which commonly used product lowers ezetimibe concentrations?","choices":["Antacids","Grapefruit juice","Alcohol","Salt substitutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antacids decrease ezetimibe concentrations, as bile acid sequestrants may; cyclosporine increases them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Where in the gut do resins block the reabsorption of bile acids?","choices":["The terminal ileum","The stomach","The brush border of the jejunum","The gallbladder wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bile acids are normally reabsorbed at the terminal ileum and recirculated to the liver; resins block that reabsorption so bile acid excretion rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When should a bile acid sequestrant be taken in relation to meals?","choices":["At least 4 hours after meals","Within 1 hour of a meal","Fasting, at bedtime only","With or without meals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins are taken within 1 hour of a meal, when bile acids are being secreted, and other medications are separated from them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Statins, ezetimibe, bile acid sequestrants and PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) through which shared end result?","choices":["Less VLDL secreted by the liver","More active hepatic LDL receptors","More HDL made by the liver","Less bile acid in the intestine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Statins and resins upregulate them, ezetimibe raises their expression by cutting cholesterol supply, and PCSK9 inhibitors keep them active longer. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which condition is an absolute contraindication to bile acid sequestrants?","choices":["Pregnancy in the third trimester","Childhood or adolescence","Chronic hepatic disease","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia (with its increased triglycerides) and triglycerides above 400 mg/dL are absolute contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which laboratory change occurs when ezetimibe is combined with a statin?","choices":["Raised hepatic transaminases","Raised serum potassium","Raised serum uric acid","Raised serum calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe's adverse effects are gastrointestinal effects and elevated hepatic transaminases when combined with statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How is ezetimibe typically used?","choices":["As first-line monotherapy","For triglycerides over 1000","As a weekly injection","Added to a statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It pairs with a statin for dual inhibition -- the statin blocks synthesis, ezetimibe blocks absorption -- for additional LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient has taken cholestyramine for years. Which deficiency is she at risk of?","choices":["Potassium, magnesium and zinc","Water-soluble B vitamins and iron","Fat-soluble vitamins and folate","Sodium, chloride and bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Resins cause malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E and K and of folic acid. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is a PCSK9 inhibitor?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Fenofibrate","Evolocumab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the PCSK9 inhibitors; the -mab suffix marks them as monoclonal antibodies. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which adverse effect profile is typical of bile acid sequestrants?","choices":["Flushing, itching, raised uric acid","Myalgia, headache, raised enzymes","Bloating, flatulence, constipation","Gallstones, nausea, myopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Resins cause gastrointestinal effects -- bloating, flatulence, fullness, constipation and nausea -- plus malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins and folic acid. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Why are bile acid sequestrants approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy?","choices":["They are cleared quickly by the kidney","They are not absorbed from the gut","They have no effect on other drugs","They have no gastrointestinal effects"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because resins stay in the gastrointestinal tract, systemic side effects are limited; they are called the safest drug for that reason. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How should ezetimibe be taken in relation to meals?","choices":["Within 1 hour of a meal","Only on an empty stomach","With or without meals","Only with a high-fat meal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe can be taken with or without meals, unlike bile acid sequestrants, which are taken within 1 hour of a meal. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which statement describes the place of bile acid sequestrants in therapy?","choices":["Most efficacious and best tolerated","First choice when triglycerides are over 1000 mg/dL","Safest, but poorly tolerated","Injectable only and expensive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are the safest because they have no systemic effects, but poorly tolerated; they are used with a statin or where only modest LDL (low-density lipoprotein) reductions are needed. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"}]};
